      "text": "First, we need to determine how many miles Jana can walk in one minute. Given that it takes her 24 minutes to walk one mile, we can calculate the miles per minute as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Miles per minute} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how many miles Jana can walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying the miles per minute by the number of minutes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Miles in 10 minutes} = \\left( \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this expression:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Miles in 10 minutes} = \\frac{10 \\text{ minutes}}{24 \\text{ minutes/mile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Miles in 10 minutes} = \\frac{10}{24}\n\\]\n\nTo convert this fraction to a decimal, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} \\approx 0.4167\n\\]\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, this is:\n\n\\[\n0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is \\boxed{0.4} miles.",
