      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by understanding her walking rate. We know that Jana walks one mile in 24 minutes. Therefore, her walking rate is \\(\\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We can do this by multiplying her walking rate by the time she will walk:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo express this as a decimal, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.4167\n\\]\n\nRounding 0.4167 to the nearest tenth gives us 0.4. Therefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
