      "text": "First, we need to determine Jana's walking speed in miles per minute. Since it takes her 24 minutes to walk one mile, her speed is:\n\\[\n\\text{Speed} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far Jana can walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her speed by the time she walks:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = \\left(\\frac{1}{24} \\text{ miles per minute}\\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nPerform the multiplication:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 10 and 24, which is 2. We then divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo convert this to a decimal, we perform the division:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{12} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
